  <!-- Scientific Foundations & Mathematical Derivations Section -->
  <div class="mb-12 border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 bg-surface-soft">
    <div class="mb-6">
      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-tight">
        {d.scienceTitle}
      </h2>
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-muted mt-1 leading-relaxed">
        {d.scienceSub}
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
      <!-- Equation 1: Cavitation Number & Rayleigh-Plesset Collapse Dynamics -->
      <div class="bg-surface border border-hairline rounded-xl p-5 shadow-2xs space-y-3">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm sm:text-base flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2.5 h-2.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-600"></span>
          1. Hydrodynamic Cavitation Number &amp; Bubble Collapse
        </h3>
        <p>
          Controlled hydrodynamic cavitation initiates when localized fluid pressure drops below vapor pressure (<span class="font-mono">P &lt; P_v</span>). The intensity is governed by the dimensionless cavitation number (<span class="font-mono">&sigma;</span>):
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg font-mono text-center text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-950 font-bold overflow-x-auto">
          &sigma; = (P_downstream - P_v) / (0.5 &middot; &rho; &middot; v_tip&sup2;)
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted">
          For micro-cavitation bubble collapse, the classical Rayleigh-Plesset equation predicts localized peak pressures exceeding 1.8 GPa and nano-scale shockwave velocities &gt; 1,000 m/s:
        </p>
        <div class="p-2.5 bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg font-mono text-center text-xs text-muted overflow-x-auto">
          R &middot; (d&sup2;R/dt&sup2;) + 1.5 &middot; (dR/dt)&sup2; = (1/&rho;) &middot; [P_int(R) - P_&infin;(t) - 2&gamma;/R - 4&mu;(dR/dt)/R]
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Equation 2: Adler-Nissen Degree of Hydrolysis (DH%) Kinetics -->
      <div class="bg-surface border border-hairline rounded-xl p-5 shadow-2xs space-y-3">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm sm:text-base flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2.5 h-2.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-600"></span>
          2. Degree of Hydrolysis (DH%) Adler-Nissen Kinetics
        </h3>
        <p>
          The percentage of cleaved peptide bonds in the target protein substrate is modeled using the standardized Adler-Nissen chemical titration kinetics:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg font-mono text-center text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-950 font-bold overflow-x-auto">
          DH% = [B &middot; N_b / (&alpha; &middot; M_p &middot; h_tot)] &times; 100%
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted">
          Where <span class="font-mono">B</span> is base titrant consumption, <span class="font-mono">N_b</span> is base normality, <span class="font-mono">&alpha;</span> is average degree of &alpha;-amino dissociation, <span class="font-mono">M_p</span> is protein dry mass (g), and <span class="font-mono">h_tot</span> is total peptide bond equivalents (e.g., 7.8 meq/g for soy glycinin, 8.0 meq/g for pea legumin).
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Equation 3: Enhanced Michaelis-Menten Biocatalytic Kinetics -->
      <div class="bg-surface border border-hairline rounded-xl p-5 shadow-2xs space-y-3">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm sm:text-base flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2.5 h-2.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-600"></span>
          3. Cavitation-Enhanced Michaelis-Menten Kinetics
        </h3>
        <p>
          Micro-bubble collapse untangles rigid globular protein folds, drastically increasing active substrate surface area and decreasing apparent Michaelis constant (<span class="font-mono">K_m</span>):
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg font-mono text-center text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-950 font-bold overflow-x-auto">
          v = (V_max^cav &middot; [S]) / (K_m^cav + [S])
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted">
          Hydrodynamic micro-shearing elevates <span class="font-mono">V_max^cav</span> by 240% to 350% while reducing <span class="font-mono">K_m^cav</span> by 40%, eliminating the traditional substrate diffusion bottleneck of un-cavitated batch vessels.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Equation 4: Non-Thermal Enzyme Protection & Specific Energy Consumption -->
      <div class="bg-surface border border-hairline rounded-xl p-5 shadow-2xs space-y-3">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm sm:text-base flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2.5 h-2.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-600"></span>
          4. Non-Thermal Enzyme Preservation &amp; SEC Energy
        </h3>
        <p>
          Enzyme thermal deactivation rate (<span class="font-mono">k_d</span>) follows first-order Arrhenius kinetics. HC-AEH maintains continuous cooling to stay beneath denaturation energy:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg font-mono text-center text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-950 font-bold overflow-x-auto">
          SEC = (P_rotor + &Delta;P_hydraulic &middot; Q) / (Q &middot; 3600)  [kWh/m&sup3;]
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted">
          By completing biocatalysis in 25 minutes instead of 4 hours, overall thermal and pumping energy consumption is reduced by 62% per metric ton of hydrolyzed protein isolate.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Engineering Reference Table -->
  <div class="border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 bg-surface-soft overflow-x-auto">
    <div class="mb-6">
